2. **Error classification and retry rules allow quality outcomes to affect inclusion and validity.** The `{0,1}` requirement in [007.md:36](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/eval/plans/007.md:36) misclassifies legitimate agent failures. Stock `Evaluator.evaluate_task` returns `resolved=False, test_exit_code=-1` when the agent finishes without a patch; verification never runs ([installed evaluate.py:324](/home/ubuntu/gemma-local/venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/swegemma/evaluate.py:324)). I reproduced this branch in memory; the timing wrapper classifies its error as a normal agent outcome.

   Consequently, a poor smoke can block continuation, and genuine zero-quality results can satisfy the “INVALID environment” rule at [007.md:60](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/eval/plans/007.md:60). Moreover, **any** code-byte change permits discarding smoke rows ([007.md:38](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/eval/plans/007.md:38)), including an unrelated comment change after seeing failures. Freeze failure categories, retain first attempts, and restrict replacement runs to documented harness defects. Also resolve whether the primary is the stock verdict or a verdict overridden by setup flags: [lines 47–48](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/eval/plans/007.md:47) currently prescribe both.

3. **The arm-2 extension does not preserve the stated exact inference.** [007.md:63](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma/eval/plans/007.md:63) permits a second sample specifically after an inconclusive ON advantage, then reapplies the same threshold to pooled pairs. That creates an outcome-dependent second look; repeated samples of the same task also need not be independent Bernoulli discordances. Pre-register a fixed sample size or a sequential rule, and use task-level aggregation or an analysis accounting for repeated tasks. “If quota allows” also needs an objective cutoff fixed before results.

